.TH SUSPEND 2
.SH NAME
suspend \- suspend/resume a process
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "suspend(pid, flag)"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B suspend
suspends a process specified by
.I pid
if
.I flag
is non-zero, or
resumes a process specified by
.I pid
if
.I flag
is zero.
The sending and receiving
process must have the same effective user ID; otherwise this call is
restricted to the super-user.
.PP
While a process is suspended, it can only be terminated by a kill signal.
One of any other signal sent the process will be caught and acted upon
when the process is resumed; more than one of a particular signal sent a
suspended process will be ignored.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
suspend(1), kill(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A \-1 is returned if the process does not exist or if the process does not
have the same effective user ID and the user is not the super-user.
.SH NOTES
Process suspension is a feature of VENIX which is not portable to standard
UNIX.
.SH ASSEMBLER
(suspend = 50.)
.TP
PDP-11:
.nf
R0 = pid
sys suspend; flag
.fi
.TP
8086:
.nf
BX = 50
AX = pid
DX = flag
int 0Xf1
.fi
.sp
